3	// Lefthook integration.
4	//
5	// Lefthook owns .git/hooks/* and regenerates every hook from its config on
6	// each run, gated by a checksum of that config. So Entire cannot keep a
7	// wrapper there: any lefthook.yml edit, or the `lefthook install -f` that its
8	// npm postinstall runs, silently takes the file back. Worse, the reclaim
9	// happens at the top of whatever hook fires next — typically the pre-commit of
10	// the very commit being made — so repairing at turn start loses the race.
11	//
12	// The fix is to stop wanting the file. Entire writes a config of its own and
13	// registers it with one `extends` entry in Lefthook's local config; Lefthook
14	// then invokes Entire's scripts itself. Lefthook resolves `extends` at run
15	// time, so the artifacts take effect immediately — no `lefthook install`, no
16	// user action — and Lefthook's own config is never touched.
17	//
18	// Verified against lefthook 2.1.10: a config reached via `extends` can declare
19	// `scripts`, those scripts receive git's hook arguments (pre-push gets the
20	// remote and its URL), and the whole arrangement survives both
21	// `lefthook install -f` and a config edit.
